Interface defines a contract for what a class can do, without saying anything about what the class should do.
The class signs the contract with the keyword 'implements'

if a concrete(non-abstract)class is implementing an interface, the compiler will ensure that the class has implementation code for each
abstract method.

As of java 8, it is possible to inherit concrete methods from interfaces. And interfaces can contain two types
of concrete methods i.e. static and default.
    1. Implementation class are not required to implement concrete methods.
    2. The 'default' interface methods are inheritable, while the static interface methods are not.

All the interfaces methods are implicitly public.
All interface methods are implicitly abstract.
    - unless they are default or static, i.e. concrete

All variables declared in an interface must be public, static and final i.e. interfaces can only declare constants
but not instance variables.

As with abstract classes, you can not 'new' an interface, but they can be used as references.

Printable p = new Printer(); //Printable is an interface.
    In this case, the class Printer implements Printable interface.

Functional interface is an interface that has only one abstract method, also called SAM(Single Abstract Method)
    - default/static methods do not count
    - method inherited from objects do not count

A lambda only works with functional interfaces.
Lambda is an instance of a class that implements a functional interface.